## Why This Is Asked

Testing cache behavior is a common interview topic because it validates several practical skills: understanding TTL semantics, handling time in tests, and thinking about race conditions. Employers want to see you can plan tests that cover both happy paths and edge cases.

## Key Concepts

- **TTL (Time-To-Live):** Understanding when cached data expires and how to verify expiration
- **Cache Stampede:** Preventing multiple simultaneous requests from hitting the backend
- **Fake Timers:** Controlling time in tests to avoid waiting for actual expiration
- **Deterministic Testing:** Using mock data to produce reliable, repeatable results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Jest/Vitest with fake timers
jest.useFakeTimers();

describe('User Cache', () => {
  const backend = new MockBackend();
  const cache = new InMemoryCache(60); // 60 second TTL

  test('populates cache on first request', async () => {
    const result = await cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'));
    expect(backend.callCount).toBe(1);
    expect(result).toEqual(backend.response);
  });

  test('returns cached value within TTL', async () => {
    await cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'));
    const cached = await cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'));
    expect(backend.callCount).toBe(1); // backend called only once
  });

  test('expires after TTL', async () => {
    await cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'));
    jest.advanceTimersByTime(61000); // 61 seconds
    await cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'));
    expect(backend.callCount).toBe(2); // cache expired, backend called again
  });

  test('handles concurrent requests (no stampede)', async () => {
    const promises = Array(10).fill().map(() => 
      cache.get('user1', () => backend.fetch('user1'))
    );
    await Promise.all(promises);
    expect(backend.callCount).toBe(1); // only one backend call
  });
});
```

## CI Considerations

Run these tests in your CI pipeline using Jest or Vitest—both support fake timers out of the box. For true concurrency testing, consider adding a separate integration test with real timers that spawns multiple processes or threads to simulate production load.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-sensitive data sharing across tenants, with explicit consent, retention policies, and data residency constraints. This assesses end-to-end testing, data lineage, masking, and the ability to fail closed (rollback) on privacy signals.

## Key Concepts
- Privacy-preserving data sharing
- Consent management and per-tenant gating
- End-to-end and contract testing across API, event bus, and warehouse
- Data lineage, masking, retention and residency rules
- Drift detection and rollback/kill-switch criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch: verify per-tenant consent gating before data is emitted
test('consent_gate prevents emission when not given', () => {
  const tenant = {id:'t1', consent:true};
  const data = extractMetrics(tenant);
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(true);
  tenant.consent = false;
  expect(emitAllowed(tenant, data)).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data-residency constraints across regions with a multi-region warehouse?
- How would you simulate a privacy violation and verify rollback gates and audit logs?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes the ability to plan end-to-end testing for a real-time, multi-tenant ML inference service where adapters are loaded per tenant on shared GPUs. It asks for concrete tests that cover isolation, latency budgets, privacy, and resilience during resource contention, mirroring production constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant adapters
- Cold-start latency and latency budgets
- Privacy and data masking in streaming inferences
- GPU contention, scaling, and graceful degradation
- Canary/incremental rollout and rollback criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple per-tenant adapter isolation test scaffold
const adapters = new Map();
function loadAdapter(tenant){
  if(!adapters.has(tenant)) adapters.set(tenant, {ts: Date.now(), ref: {}});
  return adapters.get(tenant);
}
function handle(tenant){
  const a = loadAdapter(tenant);
  return {tenant, ok: !!a, started: a.ts};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure cold-start latency across tenants with different adapter sizes?
- How would you automate GPU contention scenarios and verify rollback correctness?
